#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415927c9-b040-5af0-b297-45d7076e068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663013} {:answer/id #uuid "54082505-0665-514d-a523-921f99ec2ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663033} {:answer/id #uuid "61dbf510-346b-56f3-b256-bfd627248691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663053} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2ead06-c951-5d0f-a8b5-9e093101a9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663111} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3b298-9faf-5e5a-a94e-d36ab4bcd4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663184} :answer-field/id #uuid "09be577f-9e54-5011-a507-8d65f5469601" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "956f2b9b-03fc-555e-a10d-fbfcfc3c9ec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71114" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $EH$ of the $△\\,DEF$, if $D(2,1,2)$, $E(2,-3,4)$, $F(1,0,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $EH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DEF$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot DF\n$$\n\nSo, to find $EH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(2i-3j+4k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(i+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)i-(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(0\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-4))k \\\\  & =-2i-2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{2}=\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $DF$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DF & =|\\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot DF,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot DF \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =EH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ EH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ EH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6526c321-5e9b-5732-a20b-aed70c1e6698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663059} {:answer/id #uuid "74a19b50-6e0c-5531-8734-87dca72fa111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{66}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663075} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce44e28-47c8-5b07-829b-6dab7e0fee29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663089} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5d5692-9725-50f9-98cf-9f9da5bf6097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663106} {:answer/id #uuid "94df6a0b-81b7-5000-95ad-5b10630fcc17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{66}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663075} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c5eacb-2814-5148-be71-dbcdc32e9858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9614c1d2-7049-5594-979e-97607113e77f" :question/math-academy-id "q-128001" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(3,2,0),$ $B(4,4,1),$ $C(4,3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+4j+k)-(3i+2j) \\\\  & =i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+2k)-(3i+2j) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)j+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{66}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf3797d-9cfe-516d-93a6-e21794bff1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663007} {:answer/id #uuid "6e612863-0c2b-5e0a-8650-f53430fb63b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663065} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0343e2-3a79-5dd1-b181-0ac153169c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663067} {:answer/id #uuid "72d3bee3-6283-5a57-bf2a-611089e00db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663071} {:answer/id #uuid "be765e09-d18e-51e0-a64e-87e472f2be6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663007} :answer-field/id #uuid "1efade8d-f5e1-54ec-aed5-6c0d96b19f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da2d5243-7dca-5495-a89d-a46d7a8fab36" :question/math-academy-id "q-127863" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-1,1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,1,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 6-1\\cdot 0)i-((-1)\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 0)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =6i+6j-5k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{97}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62427f0f-a3d5-5b5f-89f8-20420a16bd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663056} {:answer/id #uuid "82fdf62a-6d04-5316-8db4-19a4a6ffb40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663092} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae5634c-114d-5a43-8c04-ed88a07ad95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663134} {:answer/id #uuid "cd335b43-a6b5-5a7c-af87-1f22fc535913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663204} {:answer/id #uuid "faf0ec94-bb7c-5744-801d-f970d4d54806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663056} :answer-field/id #uuid "17244fae-2381-5636-9c5d-f41a072e5b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e2061b85-6299-547f-bdb6-d75d6dca7169" :question/math-academy-id "q-127914" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,LMN,$ if $L(5,4,2),$ $M(9,2,5),$ $N(1,5,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(9i+2j+5k)-(5i+4j+2k) \\\\  & =4i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(i+5j+k)-(5i+4j+2k) \\\\  & =-4i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 3)i-(4\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 3)j+(4\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-i-8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-8,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c691cbf-d3a7-56be-9269-9915617e50e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674194} {:answer/id #uuid "72f1279a-9b66-52a1-8b7f-0adc29014b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674205} {:answer/id #uuid "a6911744-3cc0-580b-bfcf-73c927ca18e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd034e13-d7d0-570b-8247-6c28837fa17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674217} {:answer/id #uuid "fe37c4cf-81b2-5e7d-a4ac-216d9373b83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674205} :answer-field/id #uuid "55213294-9c90-5c12-b6da-b4a46e8e1b70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bbd5512-c745-5506-9589-e476017d1f98" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+(- \\frac{64}{3}))-((-64)+(\\frac{64}{3}))) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{256}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1931099c-2d1b-5af8-80f7-090cbab249db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674192} {:answer/id #uuid "4731326e-9b9a-5ff6-aff3-587c51ebaef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674202} {:answer/id #uuid "a8be08e9-1501-58b0-8bd0-b24641111816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674210} {:answer/id #uuid "e0af6abf-9221-5426-a3e2-2fa1d2bc4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674219} {:answer/id #uuid "f915a4c0-c1a0-5652-8118-969be9da31e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674202} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54915ac-6f13-58bd-a737-8c93b908a255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2180d312-6f4f-5d5b-86a4-02dad7f687e5" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+(- \\frac{8}{3}))-((-8)+(\\frac{8}{3}))) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32399fa1-20c9-5361-9d96-1f602b22d25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674195} {:answer/id #uuid "3744cd2b-88d1-57a5-a1d8-0dcb12bb78fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674198} {:answer/id #uuid "37aec481-2bc1-550b-93f1-2b0994165854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674199} {:answer/id #uuid "8b45065e-84be-59b1-9c57-ddb9e4cda6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674208} {:answer/id #uuid "cae629d2-0e63-5ef8-b6e5-875bb407408a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674198} :answer-field/id #uuid "3588ef6d-be33-5b91-9ae2-77a70c5b4721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "596a3028-8196-5628-a399-013186bd1729" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=25.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=25.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 5,25).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 25\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca54518b097929ecf8756bc699c5fef32de07b174134781e778fdcc395e964fe.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{25}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[v]_{u^{2}}^{25}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 25 u]_{-5}^{5} \\\\ &=1(((125)+(- \\frac{125}{3}))-((-125)+(\\frac{125}{3}))) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{500}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{500}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11db1db5-5a0f-5fb0-a754-1b0a0fcb5fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674191} {:answer/id #uuid "3931285c-aa25-5248-91c8-d02a7c61aa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674200} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbfebff-3fb6-5acc-83eb-383404c2df9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674207} {:answer/id #uuid "af393de9-3626-52b1-b53c-f870b9f3bd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674212} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2f462-d03e-54dd-8a98-bb5737baebc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674191} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c37a21-b63d-5674-9fe8-7c4cc3fd9598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bfbb3e6e-4a50-5fa2-b9fd-b83c3046c21c" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=36.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's d